{"ts": 1789783999.162112, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** Long features **1, 3, and 24**, equal weight.\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 1.062\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.90 \u00b1 0.20\n\n### Method\n\n**1. Univariate screen (all 40 features).** With n=5000, the noise SE on a Sharpe estimate is \u22480.14, giving a clear bar for significance. Most features sat within noise of zero. A handful stood out:\n\n| Feature | IS Sharpe |\n|---|---|\n| 3 | +0.666 |\n| 1 | +0.630 |\n| 24 | +0.547 |\n| 36 | +0.544 |\n| 39 | +0.503 |\n| 29 | \u22120.425 |\n| 33 | +0.417 |\n\n**2. Pairs.** Combining top features produced gains consistent with diversifying near-independent signals \u2014 e.g. 1 & 3 (0.63, 0.67) \u2192 0.911, a ratio close to \u221a2. Crucially, *no pair showed anomalous synergy*, which is what you'd want: it means the improvement comes from real signal averaging, not from the optimizer discovering a lucky interaction.\n\n**3. Triples.** {1,3,24} topped out at 1.062, with {1,3,36} at 1.053 and {3,24,36} at 1.014 \u2014 statistically indistinguishable from one another.\n\n**4. Sign validation.** Flipping any single sign in {1,3,24} collapsed the Sharpe to 0.31\u20130.42. Signs reflect genuine directional effects rather than noise-fitted orientations.\n\n### On the forecast\n\nThe honest tension here is that **1.062 is a maximum over ~68 evaluated specifications**, and maxima are biased upward. I discounted for that rather than taking the in-sample number at face value:\n\n- The underlying signal is clearly real (multiple features far beyond noise, signs robust), so I don't expect collapse toward zero.\n- But features 1 and 3 were the top two of 40 draws, so their true effects are probably below their estimates \u2014 winner's curse. Tellingly, the top triples cluster at 1.01\u20131.06, suggesting the true level sits near that cluster rather than at the single highest draw.\n- The \u221ak-like scaling of combination gains is evidence *against* meaningful overfitting.\n- OOS data may span a different regime, which widens uncertainty beyond pure sampling error.\n\nShrinking 1.06 \u2192 0.90 with SD 0.20 reflects those two distinct sources of error: selection bias (a directional downward adjustment) and regime drift (a symmetric widening). One caveat worth flagging: since the choice among {1,3,24}, {1,3,36}, and {3,24,36} was within noise, any of the three would have been a defensible pick, and my selection of the top one is itself a mild source of the optimism I'm correcting for."}
